<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Eguafo</h1><p class="st-meta">1450 CE – 1700 CE (approx.) · capital Eguafo</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Coastal Akan kingdom of the central Gold Coast whose lands held Elmina, where the Portuguese built their great fort in 1482.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Eguafo, called Commany by Europeans after the port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/komenda">Komenda</a>, was a small <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akan">Akan</a> kingdom inland from the central <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/gold-coast-colony">Gold Coast</a>. It already existed when the Portuguese arrived in 1471, and the fishing town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elmina">Elmina</a> lay within its territory, though its own chiefs dealt independently with the newcomers.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Eguafo's rulers profited from the trade in gold, and later slaves, passing through the coastal towns, charging rents and customs from European forts. In the 17th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a>, English and others competed for its favour, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/komenda-wars">Komenda Wars</a> from 1694 to 1700 drew Eguafo into conflict between the Dutch and English companies.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Weakened by those wars and by rising inland powers such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fante">Fante</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ashanti-empire">Ashanti Empire</a>, Eguafo lost importance in the 18th century. Its people became part of the Fante coastal states, and Eguafo survives as a traditional area in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana's</a> Central Region.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 1450 CE – 1700 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">West Africa</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:88.56%;width:4.98%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1450 CE – 1700 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Eguafo</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Akan religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Fante</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Ghana</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Eguafo, Commany</dd></dl></aside></div>

